From ba4877ac24d3fa10fb1c7730cb70fe3ad2699002 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Rafe Walker <45343084+rafe-walker@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 11:52:13 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] fix(core): annotate pods_run as destructive and never
 take over existing objects

pods_run was annotated destructiveHint false, so it stayed in the tool
list under disable_destructive (the only non-read tool that did) and
confirmation rules keyed on the annotation never matched it. Yet it
force-applies its Pod, Service and Route: given the name of an existing
Service that exposes the same port, it took that Service over, replacing
its type (dropping a nodePort) and its whole selector, so the Service's
traffic went to the new Pod. With a different port the merged Service
was invalid and the call failed after the Pod had been created.
resources_create_or_update, which uses the same apply, is annotated
destructive.

pods_run now:
- checks, before the first write, every object it is about to apply; an
  existing Pod, Service or Route of that name that pods_run did not
  create (managed-by label and name label) makes the call fail with an
  error naming it, and nothing is created or changed. Objects created
  by an earlier pods_run call with the same name are re-applied as
  before;
- is annotated destructiveHint true, because that re-apply replaces the
  fields of the earlier Pod, Service and Route. It is therefore hidden
  under disable_destructive;
- declares the get verb it now uses in its RBAC metadata.

The tool catalog snapshots change accordingly.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
